Installation and Configuration of Virtualization using KVM (Kernel-based Virtual Machine)
1. Prerequisites
A system with Linux (preferably Ubuntu/Debian/CentOS)
A CPU that supports hardware virtualization (Intel VT or AMD-V)
Root or sudo privileges

2. Check Virtualization Support
Run the following command to verify CPU virtualization support:

egrep -c '(vmx|svm)' /proc/cpuinfo
If output is 1 or more, virtualization is supported.
If 0, virtualization is not supported or disabled in BIOS.
To check virtualization support more clearly:
lscpu | grep Virtualization

3. Install KVM and Related Packages
For Ubuntu/Debian:
sudo apt update
sudo apt install qemu-kvm libvirt-daemon-system libvirt-clients bridge-utils virt-manager -y
 yum install qemu-kvm libvirt virt-install bridge-utils virt-manager -y

4. Start and Enable libvirtd Service
Ubuntu/Debian:
sudo systemctl enable --now libvirtd

5. Add User to libvirt Group
To use KVM without root:
sudo usermod -aG libvirt $(whoami)
Then logout and login again to apply group changes.

6. Verify Installation
Run:
virsh list --all
If KVM is properly installed, it will show a list (possibly empty) of virtual machines.

Also check:
kvm --version

7. Create a Virtual Machine Using Virt-Manager (GUI Method)
Launch Virtual Machine Manager:
virt-manager
Click “Create a new virtual machine”

Choose installation method (e.g., ISO image)

Allocate CPU, memory, and storage

Finish setup to start the virtual machine

8. Create a VM Using CLI (Optional)
virt-install \
--name test-vm \
--ram 2048 \
--disk path=/var/lib/libvirt/images/test-vm.img,size=10 \
--vcpus 2 \
--os-type linux \
--os-variant ubuntu20.04 \
--network network=default \
--graphics vnc \
--cdrom /path/to/ubuntu.iso
